          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In tumour lysis syndrome, which drug acts by directly converting existing uric a</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In tumour lysis syndrome, which drug acts by directly converting existing uric acid to the more soluble compound allantoin?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Allopurinol</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Allopurinol inhibits xanthine oxidase, blocking new uric acid production. It does not break down uric acid that has already accumulated.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Rasburicase</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Rasburicase is a recombinant uricase that directly converts existing uric acid to allantoin, which is far more soluble and easily excreted. It acts faster than allopurinol and is preferred when uric acid is already elevated.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Febuxostat</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Febuxostat, like allopurinol, is a xanthine oxidase inhibitor. It prevents new uric acid formation but does not break down existing uric acid.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Colchicine</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Colchicine is an anti-inflammatory agent used for acute gout flares. It has no role in uric acid metabolism and is not part of TLS management.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The key distinction: allopurinol blocks uric acid PRODUCTION (xanthine oxidase inhibitor), while rasburicase breaks down EXISTING uric acid to allantoin (recombinant uricase). Rasburicase acts faster and is preferred when levels are already dangerously elevated."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Rasburicase converts uric acid to allantoin enzymatically, providing rapid clearance. Allopurinol, by contrast, only prevents new uric acid formation by inhibiting xanthine oxidase."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
